content.text:
The rule is mostly about matching the size of the decision to the size of the code.

Qwik accepts ternaries, `&&`, and `if` statements. They all produce conditional JSX; none is inherently “more Qwik.” The important Qwik-specific distinction is between a conditional expression and a `component$` boundary.

A useful mental model:

```text
Tiny choice        → ternary
Present or absent  → &&
Whole render mode  → early return
Meaningful surface → component$
Repeated variant   → configuration/data
```

### 1. Ternaries are good for tiny choices

A ternary reads naturally when the question and both answers fit in your head at once:

```tsx
{busy ? "Saving…" : "Save"}
```

Or:

```tsx
color: resolved ? "green" : "yellow"
```

That is effectively a sentence:

> If it is busy, say “Saving”; otherwise say “Save.”

Turning that into a component or separate function would make the code harder to follow.

The warning sign is when the branches become substantial:

```tsx
{kind === "persona" ? (
  <div>
    {/* 30 lines */}
  </div>
) : (
  <div>
    {/* another 30 lines */}
  </div>
)}
```

Now the reader must hold two miniature interfaces in their head while locating the small differences between them. That should usually become something like:

```tsx
{kind === "persona" ? (
  <PersonaReply reply={reply} />
) : (
  <WriterReply reply={reply} />
)}
```

The conditional remains, but it now communicates the decision rather than containing both implementations.

### 2. Use `&&` when something is simply present or absent

For optional panels, there is no meaningful “else” state:

```tsx
{showOutline && <DocumentOutline />}
```

This says:

> Render the outline when it is open. Otherwise render nothing.

A ternary adds noise:

```tsx
{showOutline ? <DocumentOutline /> : null}
```

Both work identically. `&&` just better expresses the intention.

This is a good fit for dialogs, popovers, errors, loading indicators, and optional toolbar rows.

### 3. Use an early return when the whole component has one mode

An extracted panel often has one prerequisite:

```tsx
export const UserCommentPanel = component$(({ comment }) => {
  if (!comment) return null;

  return <div>{/* complete comment panel */}</div>;
});
```

That is easier than indenting the entire component:

```tsx
return comment ? (
  <div>{/* complete comment panel */}</div>
) : null;
```

The early return establishes a fact for everything below it:

> From here onward, `comment` exists.

TypeScript also understands that, so the rest of the component no longer needs `comment?.text`, `comment?.id`, and similar checks.

With Qwik hooks, the same general hook rule applies: hooks must be called consistently at the top level. Don’t conditionally skip hooks that sometimes need to run:

```tsx
// Risky
if (!comment) return null;
const state = useStore(...);
```

Prefer:

```tsx
const state = useStore(...);
if (!comment) return null;
```

### 4. Use `component$` for a meaningful interface boundary

This is the most Qwik-specific part.

A component declared with `component$` gives Qwik an independently resumable, reactive, lazy-loadable boundary. Qwik can preserve information about that boundary in the rendered HTML and load its code when needed instead of hydrating the entire application. [Qwik components](https://qwik.dev/docs/components/overview), [resumability](https://qwik.dev/docs/concepts/resumable/)

That makes components appropriate for concepts such as:

- `ManuscriptPanel`
- `CompositorPanel`
- `PersonaNotePanel`
- `SuggestionPanel`
- `UserCommentPanel`

Each has its own job, props, interactions, and reason to change.

But I would not create a component for every two-line ternary:

```tsx
const SaveButtonLabel = component$(({ busy }) => (
  <>{busy ? "Saving…" : "Save"}</>
));
```

That introduces another file or symbol, another set of props, and another concept for the reader—with no useful separation.

A good component boundary answers:

> What part of the product is this?

A poor component boundary answers only:

> Where did I move these six lines?

### 5. Use configuration for repeated variants

Consider the repeated footnote/endnote choices:

```tsx
title={kind === "footnote" ? "Footnote" : "Endnote"}
inputLabel={kind === "footnote" ? "Footnote text" : "Endnote text"}
submitLabel={kind === "footnote" ? "Insert footnote" : "Insert endnote"}
```

Each ternary is small, but collectively they describe two coherent variants. Repeating the condition risks mixing them accidentally.

A configuration object keeps each variant together:

```tsx
const NOTE_COPY = {
  footnote: {
    title: "Footnote",
    inputLabel: "Footnote text",
    submitLabel: "Insert footnote",
  },
  endnote: {
    title: "Endnote",
    inputLabel: "Endnote text",
    submitLabel: "Insert endnote",
  },
} as const;

const copy = NOTE_COPY[kind ?? "endnote"];

return (
  <TextModal
    title={copy.title}
    inputLabel={copy.inputLabel}
    submitLabel={copy.submitLabel}
  />
);
```

The code now separates two questions:

1. Which variant are we showing?
2. How is a modal rendered?

That makes adding a third note kind much safer.

### 6. Use Qwik’s class objects for conditional styling

This:

```tsx
class={`page-canvas ${
  zenMode ? "zen-mode" : ""
} ${
  paginated ? "is-paginated" : ""
}`}
```

works, but it mixes string construction with state decisions.

Qwik supports conditional class objects directly:

```tsx
class={[
  "page-canvas",
  {
    "zen-mode": zenMode,
    "is-paginated": paginated,
    "show-margin-guides": showMarginGuides,
  },
]}
```

That is easier to scan because each class is paired with its condition. It is also Qwik’s recommended approach. [Qwik conditional classes](https://qwik.dev/docs/core/styles/)

### The practical test

When reading a conditional, ask:

> Can I understand the complete decision without scrolling or mentally comparing large branches?

If yes, keep the ternary.

If the answer is no:

- Use a component when the branches are distinct UI concepts.
- Use configuration when the branches are variants of the same UI.
- Use an early return when the entire component depends on one condition.
- Use `&&` when the alternative is simply nothing.

The goal is not to eliminate ternaries. It is to ensure each ternary expresses one small decision rather than hiding a whole subsystem.

